      "title": "Annotations and Inference Mental Model",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 0,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Annotations and Inference Mental Model",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. Annotation vs Inference",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "==Annotation== = explicit type after a colon: `let n: number = 5`",
                "Types are **erased** at compile time: no runtime effect",
                "==Inference==: TS derives the type from the initializer"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Code",
                "Type"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "`let n: number = 5`",
                  "number (explicit)"
                ],
                [
                  "`let n = 5`",
                  "{blue:number}"
                ],
                [
                  "`const n = 5`",
                  "{green:5} (literal type)"
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Where to Annotate",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "{blue:Annotate} parameters: never inferred from the body",
                "{blue:Annotate} exported return types",
                "{green:Let infer} locals and callbacks",
                "==Contextual typing==: the array type gives `x` its type"
              ],
              "style": "check"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "typescript",
              "lines": [
                "export function double(xs: number[]): number[] {",
                "  return xs.map(x => x * 2); // x: number",
                "}"
              ],
              "note": "no annotation needed on x"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Common Mistake",
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "typescript",
              "lines": [
                "let x;       // implicit any (evolving any)",
                "x = 5;       // now treated as number",
                "let y: number;  // fix: annotate"
              ],
              "note": "or initialize: let x = 5"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "No initializer, no type",
              "text": "`let x;` is ==implicitly any==. Annotate it or initialize it."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Primitives, Arrays and Tuples",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 1,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Primitives, Arrays and Tuples",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. Primitives",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "{green:Lowercase} only: `string` `number` `boolean` `bigint` `symbol` `null` `undefined`",
                "Never `String` / `Number`: those are {red:wrapper objects}, not the primitives"
              ],
              "style": "check",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Arrays and Tuples",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Type",
                "Example",
                "Rule"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "`number[]`",
                  "`Array<number>`",
                  "same type, two spellings"
                ],
                [
                  "`readonly number[]`",
                  "`const r: readonly number[]`",
                  "blocks `push` and `r[0] = 2`"
                ],
                [
                  "`[string, number]`",
                  "`let p: [string, number] = ['a', 1]`",
                  "==fixed length==, type per position"
                ],
                [
                  "`[string, number?]`",
                  "`['a']`",
                  "optional last element"
                ],
                [
                  "`[name: string, age: number]`",
                  "labelled tuple",
                  "labels are documentation only"
                ]
              ],
              "frame": "dashed"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Inference",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "An array literal infers an ==array==, not a tuple",
                "`as const` gives a {blue:readonly} tuple of literal types"
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "typescript",
              "lines": [
                "const a = ['x', 1]",
                "// (string | number)[]",
                "const b = ['x', 1] as const",
                "// readonly ['x', 1]"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Mistake",
              "text": "`push` on a non-readonly tuple compiles and breaks its length guarantee. Use a ==readonly== tuple."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "any, unknown and never",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 2,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "any, unknown and never",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. any vs unknown",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "`any` ==disables checking==: every operation compiles, errors surface at {red:runtime}",
                "`any` **spreads** through assignments",
                "`unknown` = safe ==top type==: anything goes in",
                "{green:Narrow} first: `typeof`, `instanceof`, type guard"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                "any",
                "unknown"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Assign **to** it",
                  "anything",
                  "anything"
                ],
                [
                  "Assign **from** it",
                  "to everything",
                  "only `unknown`, `any`"
                ],
                [
                  "Use directly",
                  "{red:allowed}",
                  "{green:error}"
                ]
              ],
              "frame": "dashed"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "ts",
              "lines": [
                "let a: any = 'x';",
                "a.foo(); // compiles, throws",
                "let u: unknown = JSON.parse(s);",
                "if (typeof u === 'string') u.length;"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. never and exhaustiveness",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "`never` = empty ==bottom type==",
                "Return type of functions that always **throw** or loop",
                "Type left after **exhaustive** narrowing",
                "Assign the `default` leftover to `const _x: never = v`",
                "New union member becomes a {red:compile error}"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "ts",
              "lines": [
                "switch (s.kind) {",
                "  case 'circle': return 1;",
                "  case 'square': return 2;",
                "  default:",
                "    const _x: never = s;",
                "    return _x;",
                "}"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Rule",
              "text": "Untrusted input (`JSON.parse`, `catch` variables) is ==unknown==. Avoid `any`."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "void and Enums vs Union Literals",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 3,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "void and Enums vs Union Literals",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. void, undefined, never",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Type",
                "Means"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "`void`",
                  "result ignored"
                ],
                [
                  "`undefined`",
                  "result is the value undefined"
                ],
                [
                  "`never`",
                  "never returns at all"
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Callback typed `() => void` ==may return a value==, ignored",
                "Function declared `: void` {red:cannot return} one",
                "Since TS 5.1, `void` and `undefined` need no explicit `return`"
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Enums emit JS",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "{blue:Numeric} enum is ==reverse-mapped==: `E[0]` gives the name",
                "Older TS: numeric enum accepts ==any number==",
                "{green:String} enum: not reverse-mapped, ==safer==",
                "`enum` emits a runtime object",
                "`const enum` is inlined, emits nothing, breaks with ==isolatedModules=="
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "typescript",
              "lines": [
                "enum E { A, B }",
                "E[0]  // \"A\""
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Prefer union literals",
              "ink": "orange"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "`type Dir = 'up' | 'down'`: no JS, structural, ==tree-shakeable==",
                "Need runtime values? `as const` object plus `keyof typeof Obj`"
              ],
              "style": "check"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "typescript",
              "lines": [
                "const Dir = { Up: 'up', Down: 'down' } as const;",
                "type Dir = (typeof Dir)[keyof typeof Dir];"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Default",
              "text": "Union literals over enums."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Strict Mode and Common Mistakes",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 4,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Strict Mode and Common Mistakes",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. What strict turns on",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "`\"strict\": true` in tsconfig enables the ==whole family==",
                "noImplicitAny, strictNullChecks, strictFunctionTypes, strictBindCallApply, strictPropertyInitialization",
                "noImplicitThis, alwaysStrict, useUnknownInCatchVariables, strictBuiltinIteratorReturn (TS 5.6+)"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. The big three",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Flag",
                "Error when",
                "Fix"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "strictNullChecks",
                  "`null` or `undefined` used as another type",
                  "check or narrow first"
                ],
                [
                  "noImplicitAny",
                  "parameter has nothing to infer from",
                  "annotate it"
                ],
                [
                  "strictPropertyInitialization",
                  "field not set in the constructor",
                  "assign, `!` or `?`"
                ]
              ],
              "frame": "dashed"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "`string | null` forces a ==check== before use",
                "Field `!` = definite assignment, `?` = optional"
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "typescript",
              "lines": [
                "function len(s: string | null) {",
                "  return s.length; // error: possibly null",
                "}",
                "class User {",
                "  id!: number; // assigned elsewhere",
                "  nick?: string; // may stay undefined",
                "}"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Common mistakes",
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Overusing `as` casts to silence errors: ==unchecked==, only asserts",
                "Overusing non-null `x!`: unchecked, crashes at runtime if null",
                "`noUncheckedIndexedAccess` is {red:not} in strict: enable it separately"
              ],
              "style": "cross",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Remember",
              "text": "`as` and `!` assert, they never check. With `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, `arr[0]` becomes `T | undefined`."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "TypeScript Types Cheat Sheet",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 5,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "TypeScript Types Cheat Sheet",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. Annotate and Collect",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Annotate ==params== and exported returns: `function f(a: A): R`",
                "`let x: T`; let locals and callbacks ==infer==",
                "`as const` keeps literals: `readonly ['x', 1]`"
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Type",
                "Meaning"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "`T[]`",
                  "array"
                ],
                [
                  "`readonly T[]`",
                  "no push, no index assign"
                ],
                [
                  "`[A, B]`",
                  "tuple: fixed length"
                ],
                [
                  "`[a: A, b?: B]`",
                  "labelled, optional slot"
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Escape Hatches and Special Types",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Rank",
                "Tool",
                "Checked?"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "1",
                  "`unknown`",
                  "{green:safe}: narrow first"
                ],
                [
                  "2",
                  "`any`",
                  "{red:unsafe}: checks off"
                ],
                [
                  "3",
                  "`as`",
                  "assertion, unchecked"
                ],
                [
                  "4",
                  "`!`",
                  "non-null assertion, unchecked"
                ]
              ],
              "highlightRow": 0
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "`void`: return value ignored",
                "`never`: unreachable or ==exhausted== union"
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "typescript",
              "lines": [
                "default:",
                "  const _x: never = v;"
              ],
              "note": "a new union member becomes a compile error"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Choose Safely",
              "ink": "orange"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Prefer union literals: `type Dir = 'up' | 'down'`; no JS emitted",
                "`strict: true` also covers ==strictBuiltinIteratorReturn== (5.6+)",
                "Add `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` yourself: not in strict"
              ],
              "style": "check"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Remember",
              "text": "`as` and `!` only assert; they silence the compiler, they never check."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Check yourself",
      "role": "quiz",
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Check yourself",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "qa",
              "q": "With `strict` on, does `let x; x = 5; x = 'hi';` compile?",
              "a_bullets": [
                "Yes: `let x;` is an evolving ==any==, so each assignment is accepted.",
                "Fix: write `let x: number = 5;` or initialize it."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "qa",
              "q": "What is wrong with `function len(v: unknown) { return v.length; }`?",
              "a_bullets": [
                "Error: an `unknown` value cannot be used until it is narrowed.",
                "Fix: `if (typeof v === 'string') return v.length;`"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "qa",
              "q": "A `switch` on `type Dir = 'up' | 'down'` ends with `default: const _x: never = d;`. You add `'left'` to Dir. What happens?",
              "a_bullets": [
                "Compile error in `default`: `'left'` is not assignable to `never`.",
                "That error is the point: it finds the unhandled case."
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
